+# Architecture Plan: maintenance-mode
+
+Plan file: `docs/arch/maintenance-mode.md`
+
+> **Correction applied during materialization (goga-apply):** the new cell was
+> originally planned at `packages/common/maintenance`. `MaintenanceModeGuard` needs
+> `Reflector`, which lives in `packages/core/services` (`@nestjs/core`). Since
+> `@nestjs/common` has no dependency on `@nestjs/core` (verified via
+> `packages/common/package.json` — no such dependency — versus `@nestjs/core`'s
+> `package.json`, which lists `@nestjs/common` as a `peerDependency`), placing the
+> cell in `common` would create a reverse package dependency. The cell was moved to
+> `packages/core/maintenance` instead; all Imports/content below are otherwise
+> unchanged (`packages/core` can freely depend on `packages/common/exceptions`, the
+> same way `packages/core/router` already does). This is reflected in the actual
+> `packages/core/maintenance/CODEMANIFEST` and `packages/common/exceptions/CODEMANIFEST`
+> on disk, which are the authoritative artifacts; a couple of backtick-reference
+> lint fixes applied directly to those files (removing unresolvable `` `CanActivate` ``
+> and `` `Maintenance()` `` backtick links, per `goga lint`) are not re-transcribed
+> below for brevity.
+
+## Topic
+maintenance-mode — a runtime-toggleable gate that rejects marked route/WS-message
+handlers before their logic runs, using the framework's existing guard extension
+point so one mechanism covers both HTTP and WebSocket transports.
+
+## Implementation Order
+
+1. **`packages/common/exceptions`** (modify, additive only) — no dependencies of its
+   own; document the already-implemented-but-undocumented `ServiceUnavailableException`
+   type. Goes first because `packages/core/maintenance` imports from it.
+2. **`packages/core/maintenance`** (create new) — depends on
+   `packages/common/exceptions` (`ServiceUnavailableException`). Goes second.
+
+No other cells are touched. `packages/core/guards` is depended upon conceptually
+(the new `MaintenanceModeGuard` satisfies its documented `CanActivate` extension
+point) but is not modified, consistent with that cell already treating all
+consumer-defined guards as out of its own enumeration scope.
+
+## Artifacts
+
+### Cell: `packages/common/exceptions` (MODIFIED)
+
+Diff: insert one new type entry between the existing `NotFoundException` and
+`InternalServerErrorException` entries. No header changes, no other body changes,
+no footer changes.
+
+```yaml
+"HttpException::ServiceUnavailableException(objectOrError: any, descriptionOrOptions: string | Object<string, any>)":
+  location: service-unavailable.exception.ts
+  annotations: |
+    503 Service Unavailable, constructed identically to `BadRequestException`. Thrown by
+    application-defined guards (for example, maintenance-mode gating) to signal the
+    service is temporarily unable to handle the request.
+```
+
+`.usages/` files: none (unchanged).
+
+### Cell: `packages/core/maintenance` (NEW)
+
+Full CODEMANIFEST:
+
+```yaml
+Imports:
+  - Types:
+      - ServiceUnavailableException
+    From: packages/common/exceptions
+
+Annotations: |
+  Implements a runtime-toggleable maintenance-mode gate for individual handlers.
+  `Maintenance()` marks a method or class; `MaintenanceModeService` holds the in-process
+  on/off flag; `MaintenanceModeGuard` is a standard CanActivate guard (see
+  packages/core/guards) that an application registers globally so it runs — and can
+  reject — before any marked handler's logic executes, on both the HTTP and WebSocket
+  pipelines, since both already route through the same guard-resolution mechanism. This
+  cell introduces no new dispatch machinery; it is a consumer of the existing guard
+  extension point.
+
+---
+
+"MaintenanceModeService()":
+  location: maintenance-mode.service.ts
+  annotations: |
+    Holds the process-local maintenance-mode flag, defaulting to off. No persistence,
+    no cross-instance sync — flipped directly by application code (e.g. an admin
+    endpoint) via dependency injection.
+
+    Requirements: a single application-wide instance must be shared between whatever
+    toggles the flag and the MaintenanceModeGuard instance(s) checking it (i.e. provide
+    it once, inject it wherever needed — standard Nest singleton provider scope).
+  properties:
+    "enabled -> boolean": |
+      Whether maintenance mode is currently on. Defaults to false.
+  methods:
+    "enable()": |
+      Turn maintenance mode on. Takes effect immediately for the next call to any
+      marked handler.
+    "disable()": |
+      Turn maintenance mode off. Marked handlers resume normal behavior immediately.
+    "isEnabled() -> enabled: boolean": |
+      Return the current on/off state.
+
+"Maintenance() -> decorator: MethodDecorator | ClassDecorator":
+  location: maintenance.decorator.ts
+  annotations: |
+    Marks a handler method, or every handler in a controller/gateway class, as affected
+    by maintenance mode. Applied the same way at either level as `@UseGuards`:
+    method-level decoration marks one handler; class-level decoration marks every
+    handler in that class.
+
+    Algorithm: writes boolean metadata onto the decorated method or class, under a key
+    private to this cell.
+
+"MaintenanceModeGuard(maintenanceModeService: MaintenanceModeService, reflector: any)":
+  location: maintenance-mode.guard.ts
+  annotations: |
+    CanActivate implementation an application registers as a global guard (or
+    per-controller, via @UseGuards) to enforce maintenance-mode gating.
+
+    `maintenanceModeService`: the shared flag holder this guard consults on every
+    activation
+    `reflector`: standard framework Reflector, used to read the metadata Maintenance()
+    wrote, checking the handler method before falling back to its class (method-level
+    marking takes precedence, matching @UseGuards' own precedence convention)
+
+    Constraints: must resolve metadata via a handler-then-class lookup so a
+    class-level @Maintenance() marks every method in that class, and there is no
+    opt-out decorator for a single method within a marked class — marking is
+    "this handler" or "this whole controller/gateway", matching the requirement.
+  methods:
+    "canActivate(context: any) -> allowed: boolean": |
+      Returns true (allowed) unless both (a) maintenanceModeService.isEnabled() is
+      true and (b) the target (method, else class) carries Maintenance()'s metadata —
+      in which case it throws ServiceUnavailableException instead of returning false,
+      so the caller gets a clear, specific error rather than a generic guard-denial
+      ForbiddenException.
+
+      Algorithm:
+      1. If maintenance mode is off, return true immediately — zero overhead on the
+         unaffected path.
+      2. Read metadata off the handler, falling back to its class if the handler
+         itself carries none.
+      3. If unmarked, return true.
+      4. If marked, throw ServiceUnavailableException.
+
+      Requirements: must be synchronous and side-effect-free beyond reading service
+      state and metadata — this is what makes it constructible and callable directly
+      in a unit test with a hand-built context object, no server/socket required.
+
+---
+
+Author: Goga
+CreatedAt: 15/09/26
+Description: |
+  Documents the maintenance-mode gating mechanism: a decorator to mark handlers or
+  whole controllers/gateways (`Maintenance`), a runtime-toggleable flag holder
+  (`MaintenanceModeService`), and a `CanActivate` guard (`MaintenanceModeGuard`) that
+  rejects marked handlers with a clear `ServiceUnavailableException` while the flag is
+  on. Built entirely on top of the framework's existing, transport-agnostic guard
+  extension point (packages/core/guards), so the same mechanism protects both HTTP
+  route handlers and WebSocket message handlers without separate machinery.
+```
+
+`.usages/` files: none — no external library and no non-obvious multi-step consumer
+pattern beyond what the annotations above already state.
+
+## Dependency Map
+
+```
+packages/common/exceptions  (leaf, unchanged dependency set; +1 documented type)
+        ^
+        | imports ServiceUnavailableException
+        |
+packages/core/maintenance  (new leaf-adjacent cell)
+
+packages/core/guards  (unchanged; MaintenanceModeGuard satisfies its CanActivate
+                        extension point as a consumer, not a formal Import edge —
+                        same treatment as every other consumer-defined guard)
+```
+
+## Verification Checklist
+
+- `packages/common/exceptions/CODEMANIFEST`: new `ServiceUnavailableException` entry
+  present, `location:` matches the real existing file
+  (`service-unavailable.exception.ts`), constructor shape matches its siblings.
+- `packages/core/maintenance/CODEMANIFEST`: `Imports` resolves to a real type in
+  `packages/common/exceptions`; all three types have a `location:` matching the file
+  to be implemented; DSL syntax valid (`goga lint`).
+- Implementation (next stage, outside this plan): `MaintenanceModeService`,
+  `Maintenance`, `MaintenanceModeGuard` match the documented signatures exactly;
+  `index.ts` facade exports all three; guard's `canActivate` is synchronous and
+  throws the documented exception only when both conditions hold.
+- `goga contract` passes for `packages/core/maintenance` once implemented (contract
+  matches implementation).
+- Unit tests exist proving: (a) marked handler blocked while mode is on, (b) unmarked
+  handler unaffected while mode is on, (c) marked handler works again once mode is
+  off, (d) both method-level and class-level marking are honored — all without a real
+  server or socket.
